<% content_for :meta_title, "How to Find Your NTRP Level — GetCourt" %>
<% content_for :meta_description, "A practical guide to estimate your tennis level using the NTRP scale." %>

<div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 py-6">
  <div class="bg-white rounded-lg border p-6 shadow-sm">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">How to Find Your NTRP Level</h1>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
      NTRP (National Tennis Rating Program) helps players choose balanced games. You can estimate your level by consistency, shot variety, movement, and match results.
    </p>

    <div class="mb-4">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-medium text-gray-900 mb-1">Quick guide</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-gray-700 space-y-1">
        <li><strong>2.5–3.0:</strong> You can rally at a moderate pace, but consistency is limited under pressure.</li>
        <li><strong>3.5:</strong> More reliable strokes, basic tactics, and better court positioning.</li>
        <li><strong>4.0:</strong> Consistent serves and groundstrokes, directional control, and point construction.</li>
        <li><strong>4.5:</strong> Strong all-court game, pace control, advanced patterns, and high match consistency.</li>
        <li><strong>5.0:</strong> Powerful, reliable strokes with tactical flexibility and the ability to dictate points against advanced players.</li>
        <li><strong>5.5:</strong> High-level tournament player with exceptional consistency, shot variety, and match management under pressure.</li>
        <li><strong>6.0+:</strong> Elite-level competitor, typically with national/international training and results.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700">
      The best way to validate your estimate is to play several competitive matches against players with known ratings and compare results.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
